<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Educational reconstruction of the original's detail typography.

  Session 3 change: this file previously held 5 invented text appearances
  (CardTitle/CardValue/CardCaption/CardLargeValue) using `fontFamily` where the original
  uses `textFontWeight`, and #FFFFFF where the original uses #fafaff. It now reproduces
  the original's actual inheritance chain, which is the point of the exercise: the style
  names ARE the specification.

      Weather.TextAppearance.Detail
        +- .Sec       fontFamily = @string/font_sec     = "sans-serif"
        |    +- .400 / .600 / .700    android:textFontWeight
        |         +- .White / .Gray   android:textColor
        |              +- .13sp ...   android:textSize
        +- .SecNum    fontFamily = @string/font_sec_num = "clock2019L-RM"
             +- .400 -> .White -> .50dp | .70dp

  ### The two font families

  `font_sec` resolves to the literal string "sans-serif". It is NOT a bundled asset - on a
  Samsung device the platform's sans-serif IS One UI Sans, so naming sans-serif here gives
  the genuine face on the target device and a sane fallback everywhere else.

  `font_sec_num` resolves to "clock2019L-RM", the One UI clock numeral face. It is also not
  in the APK; it comes from the platform font config. Naming it has the same property: real
  on a Galaxy, graceful fallback elsewhere. This is the face of the 70dp temperature and is
  the single most recognisable piece of type on the screen.

  Neither requires copying a font file, so this whole chain is reproducible from values alone.

  ### textFontWeight, not a -medium family name

  The original selects weight with android:textFontWeight (400/600/700), which is API 28+
  variable-weight selection against one family. Writing `sans-serif-medium` instead, as the
  previous reconstruction did, picks a different family and lands on a different face.

  See reports/resource-map.md section 1.
-->
